<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mycenae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1600–1100 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a> · Also known as Mykenai, Mykene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mycenae is an archaeological site near Mykines in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolis</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the seat of the legendary King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a>, which gave its name to the Mycenaean period of Greek history, about 1600–1100 BCE. Its shaft graves of the 17th and 16th centuries BCE held rich grave goods, and about 1350 BCE its walls were rebuilt in Cyclopean masonry, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-gate">Lion Gate</a> added about 1250 BCE. Destroyed around 1200 BCE and only partly rebuilt, it was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> in 468 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> began excavating there in 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mycenae.jpg" alt="Mycenae" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andreas Trepte · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lions-Gate-Mycenae.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
